      ":question/id": "6401fb81-f836-5c3b-b86e-df571bc3c940",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{2 x^{2}}{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 x^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 x^{2})^{\\prime}\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x^{2})\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(4 x)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{4 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x}{\\ln^2 x}\\\\ &=\\frac{2x(2\\ln x-1)}{\\ln^2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(e)&=\\frac{2(e)(2\\ln (e)-1)}{\\ln^2 (e)}\\\\ &=\\frac{2 e}{1}\\\\ &=2 e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2 e.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ca833848-e6be-5c1e-95cf-032cb42bf197",
